.TH XTOP 1
.SH NAME
xtop, top \- show the processes using the machine, kept up to date
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xtop
[\ \-b\ ] [\ \-c\ |\ \-m\ ] [\ \-d\ seconds\ ] [\ \-n\ times\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xtop
(XENIX top) fills the screen with the state of the machine and redraws
it every few seconds: at the top, a meter for each of the processor, the
memory, the swap area and each mounted file system, with a summary
beside them,
.PP
.nf
 CPU[||||||||             31.0%]    Processes: 15, 1 running
 Mem[|||||||||||||||| 394K/640K]    Uptime: 2 days, 08:58:12
 Swp[|||||||          457K/1.6M]    Swapped out: 9   last 3s: 0 out, 1 in
 Dsk[||||||||||        6.8M/18M]    /: 11M free
                                    FPU: 8087   Video: CGA 80x25
.fi
.PP
and below, a line for each process, the busiest first.
.B top
is another name for it.
.PP
.B CPU
is the share of the processor's time since the last screen that the
processes used, their own times added up: the part spent in their
programs and the part spent in the kernel for them. The percentage is
the two together.
.B Mem
is the memory in use, of all the machine has, in three parts: the memory
of the processes in memory; the rest of what XENIX has given out, which
is the text of the programs running, shared by all the processes running
each one; and the kernel's: its code, its tables and its buffers, all
that XENIX keeps from the processes. On an XT with 640K the kernel keeps
about 240K.
.B Swp
is the swap area in use, of the one made when XENIX was installed: the
part of the hard disk to which XENIX moves whole processes when memory
is full, and from which it brings them back to run.
.B Swapped out
beside it is how many processes are out there now (a 0 in the column
RES below), and, since the last screen, how many XENIX moved out and how
many it brought back in, with the seconds since that screen; many
moves each time mean more is running than fits in memory.
.B Dsk
is a mounted file system's space in use, of its size, with its free
space beside it; there is a meter for each of the first two file systems
in /etc/mnttab, looked at every 30 seconds.
.PP
.B Uptime
is the time since the system was started, in days, hours, minutes and
seconds, counted in the clock's ticks, so setting the date does not
change it.
.B FPU
says whether the machine has an 8087, found by asking one to initialize
itself:
.B 8087
or
.BR none .
.B Video
is the console's display adapter and the screen's size, when xtop runs
on the console:
.B CGA
for a color display;
.B MDA
or
.B Hercules
for a monochrome one, told apart by the vertical retrace that a Hercules
card shows in its status port and an IBM monochrome adapter does not.
.PP
The columns of the processes are:
.TP 10
.B PID
the process's id
.TP
.B USER
its owner
.TP
.B TTY
its terminal, as ps(1) shows it:
.B co
for the console,
.B 02
for /dev/tty02,
.B 1a
for /dev/tty1a, and
.B ?
for none
.TP
.B PRI
its priority; the lower, the sooner it runs
.TP
.B NI
its nice value, as nice(1) set it
.TP
.B SIZE
its size, in kilobytes or megabytes: its data, its stack and the
kernel's record of it, but not its program's text, which it shares
.TP
.B RES
how much of it is in memory: all of it, or 0 when it is swapped out
.TP
.B S
its state:
.B R
running or ready to,
.B S
sleeping,
.B W
waiting,
.B I
being made,
.B Z
ended, with its parent yet to wait for it (a zombie),
.B T
stopped
.TP
.B CPU%
its share of the processor since the last screen
.TP
.B TIME+
the processor's time it has used, in minutes, seconds and hundredths
.TP
.B COMMAND
the name of the program it runs
.PP
Process 0 is the swapper, which moves processes between memory and the
swap area.
.SH THEMES
.B xtop
has two looks, Color and Monochrome.
.TP 10
.B Color
The parts of each meter in the console's colors: in programs green, in
the kernel red; the processes' memory green, the shared text yellow
and the kernel's blue; the swap area red and the disks blue. The heading
is black on green and the keys' letters bright cyan. Color needs the
console with a color display (a terminal of type ansi), and is the first
choice there.
.TP
.B Monochrome
White on black, as on a monochrome display: the heading in reverse
video, the meters and the keys' letters bright. On a monochrome display
it is the first choice and the only one; on a terminal other than the
console, only the heading stands out, in the terminal's standout mode.
.PP
The key
.B c
changes from one to the other, and the choice is kept in the file
.I .xtoprc
in the home directory, as the line
.B "theme color"
or
.BR "theme mono" ,
for the next time.
.SH KEYS
While
.B xtop
runs, these keys work:
.TP 10
.B P
sort by the processor's time since the last screen (the first order)
.TP
.B M
sort by size
.TP
.B T
sort by the time used in all
.TP
.B N
sort by process id
.TP
.B k
send a process a signal: xtop asks for its id and then the signal, 15
when none is given; 15 asks the process to end, 9 ends it
.TP
.B d
set the seconds between screens, from 1 to 25
.TP
.B c
change the theme, Color or Monochrome
.TP
.B h
show the keys
.TP
.B q
quit; the interrupt key quits too
.PP
Any other key redraws the screen at once. The bottom line shows the keys,
and the right end of the heading the order of the processes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 10
.B \-b
Print each screen after the last, without moving about the terminal,
for a file or a pipe.
.TP
.B \-c
Color, this time, whatever
.I .xtoprc
says.
.TP
.B \-m
Monochrome, this time.
.TP
.BI \-d " seconds"
The seconds between screens, from 1 to 25; 3 if not given.
.TP
.BI \-n " times"
Stop after this many screens.
.SH EXAMPLES
To save one screen in a file, taken over 5 seconds:
.PP
.nf
	xtop \-b \-n 1 \-d 5 > /tmp/xtop.out
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP 16
$HOME/.xtoprc
the theme chosen last
.TP
/xenix
the kernel, for the places of its tables
.TP
/usr/lib/xtop.syms
what xtop found before: those places, while /xenix is the same file;
the terminals' names, while /dev is unchanged; and the size of memory,
until the machine is started again
.TP
/dev/kmem
the kernel's memory
.TP
/dev/mem
the processes in memory
.TP
/dev/swap
the processes swapped out
.TP
/etc/mnttab
the mounted file systems
.TP
/dev/console, /dev/tty*
the terminals' names
.TP
/etc/passwd
the names of the owners
.TP
/etc/termcap
the terminal's capabilities
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"cannot read the kernel's symbols in /xenix" when /xenix is not the
kernel that was started or cannot be read; "cannot read /dev/kmem and
/dev/mem" when xtop has lost its set-user-id; "no termcap entry" for a
terminal it does not know. The exit status is 1 after any of these, 2
after a mistake in the options, and 0 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
ps(1), kill(1), nice(1), df(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B top
command was written by William LeFebvre at Rice University in 1984.
.B xtop
is this system's, after it. Its screen follows the general look of
htop, by Hisham Muhammad. The swapping counts, the file system meters
and the FPU and video lines are its own.
.SH AUTHORS
xtop was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
On an XT xtop takes about a third of a second of the processor for
each screen, a tenth of its time at 3 seconds between screens, and
about three seconds to start, a second or two more the first time and
after the kernel changes, while it looks through /xenix. A longer delay, as
.BR "xtop \-d 5" ,
leaves more for the rest.
.PP
XENIX keeps no count of the processor's idle time or of the disk's work,
so xtop shows neither: the processor's time is the processes' own, and
the kernel's work for the clock, the disks and the terminals counts
against whatever process was running when it came. The moves to and
from the swap area are those xtop sees from one screen to the next; a
process swapped out and back in between does not show.
.PP
The 8087 has no counter of its own work, and XENIX does not keep track
of which processes use it, so xtop can say only whether there is one.
.PP
The file systems are those mounted when xtop starts; it reads them as
sysinfo, and gives that up after.
.PP
xtop reads the kernel's tables while the kernel changes them, so a
process just made or just ended can show for a screen with its name
missing.
.PP
xtop is set-user-id to sysinfo, who may read the kernel's memory and the
disks, as ps(1) and df(1) are; it gives that up once its files are open.
.PP
At most 30 lines of the terminal are used.
.PP
The uptime starts over after nearly 7 years of running without a
restart, when the count of ticks does.
